# C Style Guide

## File structure

- Begin every `.c` and `.h` file with the GPLv3 block comment (the standard
  multi-line `/* ... */` form, indented with two spaces inside).
- Follow the license block with a `/** ... */` doc comment naming the file and
  describing its purpose, plus `:author:`, `:license:`, and `:ai-assistant:`
  fields (matching the Python house style).
- End every standalone `.c` file with `// The end.` on the last line.

## Indentation and line width

- Use 2-space indentation throughout.
- Keep lines within 79 characters where practical.

## Function signatures

When all parameters fit on one line, keep them there:

```c
static void clear_and_free_transfer_plan(KosContext *ctx) {
```

When parameters would push past the line limit, break to one parameter per
line with a 4-space indent, and place the closing `)` on the last parameter
line immediately before `{`:

```c
static int run_transfer_plan_to_completion(
    KosContext *ctx,
    KosEvent *out_ev) {
```

Never use alignment-based continuation (do not align parameters to the opening
parenthesis).

## Blank line after opening brace

When a function uses the expanded (one-per-line) parameter style, leave a
blank line between the closing `){` and the first statement of the body:

```c
static KosEvalMode revive_plan(
    KosContext *ctx,
    PyObject *send_val,
    PyObject *throw_exc) {

  KosTransferPlan *plan = KosContext_PLAN(ctx);
  ...
}
```

Functions with compact single-line signatures also begin their body with a
blank line when the body is non-trivial (more than one or two statements):

```c
PyObject *KosOperator_Add(PyObject *const *values, size_t count) {

  return reduce_method("+", PyNumber_Add, PyNumber_Positive, values, count);
}
```

Tight, single-statement bodies (e.g. macro-based opcode handlers) may omit
the leading blank line.

## Blank lines within function bodies

Use blank lines to separate distinct logical phases inside a function body.
Place a blank line:

- After a variable declaration block before the first statement.
- Between sequential `if` / `if-else` guards that each represent an
  independent early-exit or branch.
- After a multi-statement block before the next unrelated step.
- Before a `return` that follows a non-trivial block.

Do not place blank lines mechanically before every statement; only insert
them where they add clarity by marking a phase boundary.

## Negated pointer and boolean checks

Use `! ptr` (with a space) rather than `ptr == NULL` or `!ptr`:

```c
/* correct */
if (! val)
  return -1;

if (! PyErr_Occurred())
  PyErr_SetString(...);

/* avoid */
if (val == NULL)
  return -1;
```

## Braces on `if` / `for` / `while`

Single-statement bodies may omit braces:

```c
if (res < 0)
  return -1;
```

Use braces when the body is multi-statement or when an `else` branch requires
them for symmetry.

## Section separators

Use banner comments to divide a file into named sections:

```c
/* === Section name === */
```

Leave two blank lines before a section banner and one blank line after it.

## Two blank lines between top-level definitions

Separate every top-level function or variable definition with exactly two
blank lines.

## Multi-line call-site continuation

When a function call must wrap, indent the continuation by 4 spaces (not
aligned to the opening parenthesis), mirroring the signature style:

```c
PyObject *frame_ns = KosFunction_PrepareCallNamespaceFromVector(
    callable, args, nargs, kwnames);
```
